WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.376
A small business can hold Bitcoin and still need working capital.

00:00:04.376 --> 00:00:09.450
Tillward connects a Bitcoin backed loan to the customer payments that help repay it.

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:17.313
The owner creates a business account, supplies Bitcoin collateral, and borrows MUSD through Mezo.

00:00:17.313 --> 00:00:24.450
The browser journey shown here used two thousand test MUSD against point zero four five test Bitcoin.

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:31.372
Next, choose the share of each final customer payment to allocate toward the loan.

00:00:31.372 --> 00:00:33.683
Set an operating cash floor too.

00:00:33.683 --> 00:00:38.450
The business can keep money available for its day to day expenses.

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:42.848
Create a final payment invoice and share its link.

00:00:42.848 --> 00:00:47.605
The customer approves the exact MUSD amount, then confirms payment.

00:00:47.605 --> 00:00:53.450
Tillward records the receipt and its repayment allocation inside the business account.

00:00:54.450 --> 00:01:00.952
With a twenty percent rule, a five hundred MUSD receipt allocates one hundred toward repayment.

00:01:00.952 --> 00:01:04.203
Four hundred stays in the account for operations.

00:01:04.204 --> 00:01:08.450
Settlement also respects the cash floor and native minimum debt.

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:12.294
That repayment path is proven on public testnet.

00:01:12.294 --> 00:01:18.753
A separate sample customer paid five hundred MUSD, and one hundred reduced the account's native Mezo debt.

00:01:18.754 --> 00:01:25.450
Later hosted settlements brought recorded totals to five hundred two collected and one hundred point four repaid.

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:30.322
The owner can inspect activity, top up funds, and close the loan.

00:01:30.322 --> 00:01:40.450
In the Rabby browser journey, the loan reached zero debt and all point zero four five test Bitcoin returned to the owner, with transaction evidence checked independently.

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:47.392
Tillward now has a public demo, guided onboarding, and hosted repayment across three enrolled accounts.

00:01:47.392 --> 00:01:51.719
The next step is testing demand with businesses willing to collect MUSD.

00:01:51.719 --> 00:01:53.450
This release uses test assets.

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:59.450
Explore Tillward, inspect the proof, and follow the code on GitHub.
